---
// /about/: the story of why rotli exists, told by its maker in first person, then the name
// (#name stays a stable anchor for the name story, though nothing links to it today). One
// reading column, no table of contents: it is a story, not a reference. The two quokkas are
// the story film's island (the opening scene, IslandScene.astro) and the app's own "stays
// local" character beside the vault.
// Every claim restates something the rest of the site already says (landing, /privacy/,
// PRODUCT.md); the maker's facts come only from his public portfolio. The longer story is the
// "creation" post, linked once it is published.
//
// [[OWNER: one real moment from your own life that made you start (a note you lost, an app that
// changed under you, a bill you did not expect). If you add it, put it as one or two sentences
// at the end of "Notes come first". The page reads true without it.]]
// [[OWNER: the true reason it is called a quokka and not something else, if there is one beyond
// "they look happy". Optional; the name section works without it.]]
import stayLocal from '../../../../src/assets/characters/filled/cocoa/stays_local.webp';
import IslandScene from '../../components/IslandScene.astro';
import WritingPage from '../../components/WritingPage.astro';
import { ogImage } from '../../og';
import { GITHUB_URL, site } from '../../site';
import { publishedWriting, slugOf } from '../../writing';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return site.showsFullSite ? [{ params: { slug: undefined } }] : [];
}

const X_URL = 'https://x.com/iamsethmedina';
// The same survey the landing page's figure cites (StatBand.astro), checked 2026-10-02.
const SURVEY_URL = 'https://www.self.inc/info/cost-of-unused-paid-subscriptions/';

const posts = await publishedWriting('post');
const story = posts.find((post) => slugOf(post) === 'the-creation-of-rotli');
---

<WritingPage
  title="About — rotli"
  description="Why Seth Medina is building rotli: a private workspace on notes you own, with AI that works under your rules and costs nothing extra. Also, where the name comes from."
  canonicalPath="/about/"
  {...ogImage('about')}
  section="about"
  heading="Why I’m building rotli"
  lede="Notes are where work starts. They should live somewhere that is yours."
  center
>
  <figure slot="scene" class="island-frame">
    <IslandScene />
    <figcaption>Rottnest Island, where the quokkas live, and where rotli gets its name.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2 id="notes">Notes come first</h2>
  <p>
    Before there is a project, there is a note. A name, a half idea, something someone said on a call. You
    type a few words so you won’t forget, and everything else gets built on top of them.
  </p>
  <p>
    So I think where your notes live matters more than almost any other choice you make about your tools.
    It is the foundation. rotli is my attempt to build a private workspace on that foundation, kept in a
    vault you own. It is a complete workspace on its own, and AI is optional.
  </p>

  <h2 id="two-kinds">People and AI take notes differently</h2>
  <p>
    I write in half sentences. <em>call w/ dana re pricing, deck fri??</em>{' '}I know exactly what that means
    today. An AI reads differently. It wants a clear title, a few tags, and links to related notes, because
    that is how it finds things again. Neither way is wrong. They are two different jobs.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most tools hand you the second job. Name it properly, tag it, write it so the assistant can follow it.
    I think that is backwards. Every minute you spend shaping your notes around an AI is a minute taken from
    the thing you sat down to do, and it breaks your flow. That attention belongs to your creative work.
  </p>
  <p>
    So in rotli you only write. If you turn it on, a Librarian files your notes in the background, adds the
    tags and links around your words, and never rewrites them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="vault">A vault you own</h2>
  <div class="vault">
    <img src={stayLocal.src} width="512" height="512" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
    <div>
      <p>
        Every note is a plain Markdown file in one folder you choose. I call that folder your vault. There is
        no account to make and no rotli server holding your notes. Delete rotli tomorrow and your files are
        right where you left them, and any other editor can open them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Search, chat, and the Librarian are all built on top of that folder. When the foundation is yours, the
        rest can change without you losing anything.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="cost">You shouldn’t pay extra to use your own notes</h2>
  <p>
    Many people already pay for an AI tool, and often more than one. One survey found about half of the
    people paying for ChatGPT hadn’t used it in the past 30 days
    (<a href={SURVEY_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Self Financial</a>). The subscriptions are there, waiting.
  </p>
  <p>
    So rotli doesn’t sell you AI, and it doesn’t add a fee for it. Run a model on your Mac, or connect the
    AI tools you already have. rotli never holds your keys.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rules">AI is welcome, under rules you set</h2>
  <p>
    I like having AI around. I just want it to follow house rules, and I want you to write them. Mark a note
    secure and only the model on your Mac can read it. Lock a note and no AI can change it. You decide, note
    by note, what each one may see.
  </p>

  <h2 id="name">Why “rotli”, and why a quokka</h2>
  <p>
    rotli is short for Rottnest, the island off Western Australia where quokkas live. Short, quick to say,
    easy to remember. The workspace is the island, and you are the quokka.
  </p>
  <p>
    Quokkas are famous for looking like they can’t stop smiling. That is the feeling I want rotli to have: a
    workspace that feels good to be in, so getting things done feels good too.
  </p>

  <h2 id="who">Who makes it</h2>
  <p>
    I’m Seth Medina, a software engineer in Florida. I taught myself to build software, and I like tools that
    get out of the way.
    {
      story && (
        <>
          {' '}The longer story is on the blog:{' '}
          <a href={`/blog/${slugOf(story)}/`}>{story.data.title}</a>.
        </>
      )
    }
  </p>
  <p>
    Two open-source editors are built in, and both save ordinary files in your folder:{' '}
    <a href="https://excalidraw.com">Excalidraw</a> draws your boards, and{' '}
    <a href="https://univer.ai">Univer</a> edits your Word documents.
  </p>
  <ul class="elsewhere">
    <li><a href="/download/">Try rotli</a></li>
    <li><a href={X_URL} rel="me">Follow along on X · @iamsethmedina</a></li>
    {site.sourcePublic && <li><a href={GITHUB_URL}>Read the source on GitHub</a></li>}
    <li><a href="/blog/">Read the blog</a></li>
  </ul>
</WritingPage>

<style>
  .island-frame {
    margin: 0 0 clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4rem);
  }
  .island-frame :global(.island) {
    aspect-ratio: 1200 / 420;
    overflow: hidden;
    border: 1px solid var(--border-strong);
    border-radius: var(--radius-frame);
    background: var(--surface-2);
  }
  figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.85rem;
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  /* The vault: the quokka beside the first paragraphs, stacked above them on narrow screens. */
  .vault {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 7.5rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    align-items: start;
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }
  .vault img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .vault {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 0.5rem;
    }
    .vault img {
      width: 6rem;
    }
  }
  .elsewhere {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.6rem 1.5rem;
    margin: 1.25rem 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }
  .elsewhere a {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
